From 3a1339974a7f5028b84ef93e7ef41d25c16391b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 20:40:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08127/11362] [outer-loop] ARC trust-gate blast radius: what
 admitting a weak engine costs

Measures the DOWNSIDE of relaxing the plain-branch world-model trust gate to
~0.2 cell_recall, which nobody had established before proposing it.

Three findings, CPU-only, 14 plain-branch games:

1. The prior `llm_on_fix_cellrecall` null is VOID, not negative. The threshold
   is a hardcoded `< 0.5` literal; CARNOT_ARC_TRUST_METRIC switches which
   quantity is compared, not what it is compared against. Max achievable
   cell_recall in that arm was 0.0476 (0.2474 corpus-wide), so 0 records could
   ever have been admitted. 11 of its 25 cells also took the hidden-state
   branch, which ignores the flag entirely. This voids a leg of the standing
   "a threshold relaxation is not the lever" conclusion.

2. The trust gate is the ONLY defence. 36/36 cells consumed every plan step;
   zero early abandonment; cost is exactly len(plan). Both E3AgentPolicy's
   class docstring and plan_in_model's docstring claim execution halts "on
   divergence" -- no such check exists. At the shipped cap of 80 that is 20%
   of a game's 400-action scored budget, and 6/14 games kept firing plan steps
   after GAME_OVER (up to 64 of 80 steps into a dead game).

3. Relaxation admitted 10/14 games; 10/10 planned; 10/10 executed fully;
   0/10 gained a level. 120 real actions, zero progress. The frontier effect
   is directionally negative but a clearly-stated NULL (sign p=0.18 against a
   pre-registered min reachable p of 1.22e-4).

guard_needed: YES. No shipped default flipped; no evidence file written
(results/arc_e3 verified byte-identical).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
